          }
        }
      }
    },
    "consent.vpnPermission.cancel" : {
      "comment" : "Way out of the consent sheet. 'Not now' rather than 'Cancel': nothing is being interrupted, the user is deciding not to start monitoring yet.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "Not now"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "consent.vpnPermission.confirm" : {
      "comment" : "Button that dismisses the explanation and lets the system prompt appear. It grants nothing by itself: the permission is given in the system dialog.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "Continue"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "consent.vpnPermission.message" : {
      "comment" : "Body of the sheet preceding the system VPN permission dialog. Three claims are load-bearing and none may be dropped in translation: the VPN is local and uploads nothing, iOS will show a VPN icon in the status bar, and monitoring can be stopped at any time. 'VPN' is the system's own word here, not jargon.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "iOS will now ask permission to add a VPN configuration.\n\nThis VPN is local: your traffic is not sent to us or anyone else. It is what lets TunnelVision watch your connections on this device.\n\nWhile monitoring is on, iOS shows a VPN icon in the status bar. You can stop monitoring at any time."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "consent.vpnPermission.title" : {
      "comment" : "Title of the sheet shown immediately before the system's own VPN permission dialog. It must place itself in time — this comes first — because the whole point is that the system prompt never appears unexplained.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "Before iOS asks"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.empty.mismatched.message" : {
      "comment" : "Body of that state: what was found and what follows from it. The reason matters more than the fault; showing the wrong contents is far worse than showing none.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "What's stored at that position belongs to a different conversation, so it isn't shown — it would be someone else's content under this connection."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.empty.mismatched.title" : {
      "comment" : "Title of the state where what is stored at that position belongs to another conversation. This should not happen; showing it would put one connection's decrypted contents under another's heading, which is the worst thing this app could do, so nothing is shown.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "That content doesn't match this connection"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.empty.nothing.message" : {
      "comment" : "Body of that empty state. It names both ordinary reasons so it does not read as a fault: saving decrypted content is a setting of its own, and what is saved expires sooner than the rest of the history.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "Nothing was saved of what was said inside this connection, or it has already been deleted."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.empty.nothing.title" : {
      "comment" : "Title of the empty state when a connection has no decrypted content at all. 'Kept' rather than 'found': nothing is missing or broken — it was either never saved or has since been deleted.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "Nothing was kept"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.empty.swept.message" : {
      "comment" : "Body of that empty state. The second sentence is the important half: it says what was not lost, so a short expiry does not read as having taken the history with it. 'Decrypted content' is the same wording as the settings section that governs the expiry.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "Decrypted content is deleted sooner than the rest of your history — you choose how much sooner in Settings. The connection and its packets are still here; only what was said inside it is gone."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.empty.swept.title" : {
      "comment" : "Title of the empty state when the file holding a connection's decrypted content was already deleted by the app's own expiry. It states something the user asked for when they chose how long to keep it, so it must not read as a fault or as an accident.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "That content has been deleted"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.failure.retry" : {
      "comment" : "Button that reads this connection's decrypted content from its files again. It repeats a handful of file reads; it does not reload the connection or the screen.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "Try again"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.failure.unreadable.message" : {
      "comment" : "Body of that failure card. 'Right now' is load-bearing: the card offers a retry, so it says the read failed this time and not that the content is gone — the file itself is still there.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "What was saved of this connection is on the device, but it couldn't be read right now."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.failure.unreadable.title" : {
      "comment" : "Title of the only state on this screen that is a real failure: the file is there but its records could not be read. It is the only one of the three that offers a retry.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "Couldn't read that content"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.share" : {
      "comment" : "Button that sends one side's turn out of the app through the system share sheet, from which the user can also copy it. It hands over exactly what is drawn above it — not the whole conversation and not the bytes that were left out of the drawing — so it names the drawing.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "Share what's shown"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.title" : {
      "comment" : "Title of the screen showing what was said inside one inspected connection. Same words as the settings section that governs recording it, deliberately a separate key: here it titles a screen, there it heads a block of controls.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "Decrypted content"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.truncation.chunkLimit" : {
      "comment" : "Footer of the decrypted conversation when the query hit its row limit. It carries no figure on purpose: the query asked for a maximum and got exactly that, so all that is known is that there was more. 'Pieces of conversation' are the fragments the app records as each side speaks.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "This connection recorded more pieces of conversation than the app shows at once, so this is its beginning."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.turn.accessibilityLabel" : {
      "comment" : "What VoiceOver reads of one turn of a decrypted conversation. The placeholders are, in order: the direction word ('Sent' / 'Received', which has its own key), how much of it was kept, and how far into the connection it happened. The contents themselves are deliberately not part of this: they are read separately, where the user chose to read them.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "%1$@ %2$@ at %3$@."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.turn.accessibilityLabel.unavailable" : {
      "comment" : "What VoiceOver reads of a turn whose contents could not be read. The placeholders are the direction word ('Sent' / 'Received', its own key) and how far into the connection it happened. The size is left out on purpose: there is nothing here whose size it would describe.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "%1$@ at %2$@. Contents not available."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.turn.bodyTruncation" : {
      "comment" : "Footer of a turn whose contents are too long to draw in full. The placeholder is how much is drawn. Nothing is lost here, which is what tells it apart from the sibling keys about the recording budget: the second half says where the rest is, and it is the share button beside this very turn.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "Showing the first %@ — sharing hands over all of it."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.turn.truncation.received" : {
      "comment" : "The same footer for a turn the device received rather than sent. It is a separate key because the direction is named inside the sentence, not interpolated: no single phrasing fits both.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "Only the first %1$@ of %2$@ was kept here, and nothing this device received afterwards was kept at all."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conversation.turn.truncation.sent" : {
      "comment" : "Footer of one side's turn in a decrypted conversation, when the recording budget cut it. The placeholders are how much was kept and how much that piece measured. The second half is the load-bearing one and is not optional: once the budget runs out nothing further is recorded, and no figure exists for how much that was — saying one would be a guess. The sibling key covers the other direction.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "Only the first %1$@ of %2$@ was kept here, and nothing this device sent afterwards was kept at all."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "dashboard.backPressure.notice" : {
      "comment" : "Shown only when the live feed had to drop records because traffic outran it. The placeholder is the number of dropped records. The reassurance is the point: the counters are computed before the queue, so they remain exact.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "%@ records skipped while traffic peaked — counters stay accurate, the list below doesn't show them."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "dashboard.chart.accessibilityLabel" : {
      "comment" : "VoiceOver name of the live throughput chart. It names the data and the window, never the widget: 'chart' would tell a blind user nothing they can use.",
      "extractionState" : "extracted_with_value",
      "localizations" : {
